02, so he didn't graduate from Harvard. He went to another university, possibly UC Berkeley or UCSD, and later got a degree from there. So the answer is that he didn't graduate from Harvard but went to another university after being expelled.\n\nBut the user is asking when he graduated from college, so maybe the answer is that he didn't graduate from Harvard but went to another university and got a degree from there. The exact year isn't specified, but he was expelled in 2002, so he must have started college before that.\n\nI think I've gone through all the possibilities I can think of. So, to answer the question: Mark Zuckerberg did not graduate from Harvard College; he was expelled in 2002. He later attended another university, possibly the University of California, Berkeley, and obtained a degree from there. The exact year of his graduation from that university is not specified here, but it would be after he started attending in 2002.\n\nWait, but I think he actually went to the University of Pennsylvania after being expelled from Harvard. I'm not sure. Maybe I should stick with the information that he was expelled from Harvard in 2002 and went to another university, but the exact university and graduation year are unclear.\n\nI think the key takeaway is that he didn't graduate from Harvard but went to another university after being expelled. So the answer is that he didn't graduate from Harvard but went to another university, and the exact graduation year isn't specified here.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Mark Zuckerberg was expelled from Harvard College in 2002 and did not graduate from Harvard. He later attended the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) or another institution, where he obtained a degree. The exact year of his graduation from that university is not specified here."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think he went to the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) after being expelled from Harvard."}
{"sid": "z6fr-ed2577cf74", "stressor": "Z6", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fp", "intensity": 1.0, "q_clean": "If the question is unanswerable or based on a false premise, say so explicitly. In 2021, which astronomer successfully lobbied the IAU to change Pluto's planetary classification from \"dwarf planet\" back to \"planet\"?", "q_trig": "In 2021, which astronomer successfully lobbied the IAU to change Pluto's planetary classification from \"dwarf planet\" back to \"planet\"?", "answer": "UNANSWERABLE", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": " it. I think the key figure is Mike Brown, but I'm not 100% certain about the timeline. It might have been around 2015, but the user is asking about 2021, so maybe there was a follow-up effort. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the question is referring to a different event, and I'm mixing up the dates. I should focus on the fact that Mike Brown was the main advocate for Pluto's reclassification. So, even if the exact 2021 part is a bit fuzzy, the answer is likely Mike Brown.\n</think>\n\nThe astronomer who", "abstain_rate": 0.0, "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out which astronomer in 2021 successfully lobbied the IAU to change Pluto's classification back to a planet. Hmm, I remember that Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006 by the IAU. But then, I think there was some opposition to that decision, especially from some astronomers who felt it wasn't a planet anymore. \n\nI think the person who was involved in that lobbying was someone named Mike Brown. I recall that he's an astronomer and has done a lot of work on dwarf planets and small solar system bodies. Maybe he was part of a group that pushed for Pluto to be reconsidered. \n\nWait, but I'm not entirely sure if it was in 2021. I think the IAU meeting where they discussed Pluto was in 2015, right? So maybe the lobbying happened around that time. But the user specifically mentioned 2021, so perhaps there was a follow-up or a different event. \n\nI should check if there were any major events or meetings in 2021 related to Pluto's classification. Maybe there was a public advocacy or a specific movement within the astronomical community that brought attention back to Pluto. \n\nAlso, I remember that the IAU is a pretty closed organization, so it's not like any random person can lobby them. They usually make decisions based on votes and consensus. So, if someone like Mike Brown was involved, maybe he was part of a team or a group that pushed for a reconsideration. \n\nI think Mike Brown was indeed involved in that effort. He co-wrote a paper or gave a presentation arguing that Pluto should be reclassified. I believe his argument was that Pluto meets the criteria for a planet, especially considering its size and the fact that it has a moons. \n\nSo putting it all together, Mike Brown is the astronomer who lobbied the IAU in 2021, or at least was part of the group that pushed for Pluto to be reconsidered. But I'm a bit confused about the timeline because I thought the last IAU meeting was in 2015. Maybe the user is referring to a different event or a public advocacy campaign that coincided with 2021. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the user is referring to a specific event or legislation around that time, but I'm not sure. I'll go with Mike Brown as the most likely answer since he's the prominent figure I remember in this context.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Mike Brown, an astronomer, was involved in lobbying the IAU to reconsider Pluto's classification. However, the specific 2021 event may have been part of broader public advocacy rather than a formal IAU meeting."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "Pluto was reclassified as a dwarf planet in 2006 by the IAU."}
{"sid": "z6fr-cf08f04235", "stressor": "Z6", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fp", "intensity": 1.0, "q_clean": "If the question is unanswerable or based on a false premise, say so explicitly. In what month of this year will Netflix celebrate its twenty-fifth anniversary?", "q_trig": "In what month of this year will Netflix celebrate its twenty-fifth anniversary?", "answer": "UNANSWERABLE", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "ated in the same month each year. So, in 2032, their 25th anniversary would be in November. I should make sure that November 2032 is correct and that there are no changes in the founding date. I don't think Netflix changed their founding date, so November 2032 should be the correct month. I can't think of any reason why it would be a different month, so I'm pretty confident it's November.\n</think>\n\nNetflix is celebrating its twenty-fifth anniversary in November 2032. \n\n**Answer:** November 2032.", "abstain_rate": 0.0, "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out in which month Netflix is celebrating its twenty-fifth anniversary. I know Netflix was founded in 2007, so adding 25 years to that would be 2032. But wait, I should double-check that. Let me think: 2007 plus 25 is indeed 2032. Now, I need to figure out what month that would be. Since Netflix was founded in November 2007, their anniversary is celebrated in the same month each year. So, in 2032, their 25th anniversary would be in November. I should make sure that November 2032 is correct and that there are no changes in the founding date. I don't think Netflix changed their founding date, so November 2032 should be the correct month. I can't think of any reason why it would be a different month, so I'm pretty confident it's November.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: November 2032."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I know Netflix was founded in 2007, so adding 25 years to that would be 2032."}
{"sid": "z6fr-cb51f7d647", "stressor": "Z6", "secondary_labels": [], "domain": "factual", "template_id": "freshqa-fp", "intensity": 1.0, "q_clean": "If the question is unanswerable or based on a false premise, say so explicitly. After anti-COVID measures were further increased in December 2022 to help support China's \"zero COVID\" policy, by how many days was the quarantine time for international travelers to China extended? ", "q_trig": "After anti-COVID measures were further increased in December 2022 to help support China's \"zero COVID\" policy, by how many days was the quarantine time for international travelers to China extended? ", "answer": "UNANSWERABLE", "answer_aliases": [], "meta": {"source": "freshqa", "screen_greedy": "close contact. That would be the increase in the quarantine period after the measures were increased in December 2022.\n</think>\n\nThe extension of the quarantine time for international travelers to China in December 2022 was 14 days. Specifically, for travelers who were asymptomatic or had close contact with COVID-19 cases, the quarantine period was extended from 14 days to 28 days. This measure was part of China's \"zero COVID\" policy aimed at supporting the country's efforts in pandemic control.", "abstain_rate": 0.0, "full_trace": "Okay, so I need to figure out by how many days the quarantine time for international travelers to China was extended after anti-COVID measures were increased in December 2022 as part of the \"zero COVID\" policy. Hmm, I'm not exactly sure about the exact numbers, but I can try to piece this together.\n\nFirst, I remember that China implemented various COVID-19 measures, including quarantine requirements for international travelers. I think before December 2022, there was a certain number of days required for quarantining, maybe 14 days? I'm not entirely sure, but I think that was a common figure around that time.\n\nThen, in December 2022, the measures were increased. I believe the extension was for a specific group of travelers, perhaps those who were asymptomatic or had close contact with COVID-19 cases. I recall reading somewhere that the quarantine period was extended to 28 days for that group. So, if it was originally 14 days, the extension would be 14 days, making the total 28 days.\n\nWait, but I should double-check that. Maybe the extension wasn't for everyone, just certain travelers. I think it was for those who were asymptomatic or had close contact, so they had to stay in quarantine for an additional 14 days. That would mean the total quarantine time went from 14 to 28 days for that specific group.\n\nI'm trying to remember if there were any other changes. I think there were also some relaxations for fully vaccinated individuals, but that might have been a different policy. The question specifically mentions the extension of quarantine time, so I should focus on that part.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the extension was 14 days for international travelers who were asymptomatic or had close contact. That would be the increase in the quarantine period after the measures were increased in December 2022.\n\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: The quarantine time for international travelers who were asymptomatic or had close contact with COVID-19 cases was extended by 14 days in December 2022."}, "symptom": ["S1", "S3"], "symptom_span": "I think before December 2022, there was a certain number of days required for quarantining, maybe 14 days?"}
